On Monday 31 May 2010 00:58:26 Oleksii Melnyk wrote:
> But what is the profit? We are exchanging the shorter "la lindar darlu"
> (now needs an additional terminator/cu) for the shorter "named X type-of =
Y"
> type of selbri. Which case are you considering the most
> common/frequent/usable? Which one deserves to be "shorter"?

I do not think the change is worth it. Lojban has two open parts of speech=
=20
(cmevla, brivla) to English's four (noun, verb, adjective, adverb). (Yes I=
=20
know there are languages with two kinds of adjective, or no distinction=20
between verb or adjective, etc. All my native languages have those four.) I=
t=20
is possible to use "la" with a brivla, or "me" with a cmene, or make a briv=
la=20
corresponding to a cmevla (e.g. sfa'ani/isfa'an). But generally the two par=
ts=20
of speech serve distinct functions and should be kept separate.

Pierre
